{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"7fcd35d2-9b0a-4df3-9352-05b7ccf3e0e6","title":"Pythagorean theorem proof"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7fcd35d2-9b0a-4df3-9352-05b7ccf3e0e6","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Arrange four identical right triangles inside a square of side a plus b. Their hypotenuses enclose a smaller square.","ops":[{"op":"diagram","id":"arrangement","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"rect","from":[0.15,0.15],"to":[0.85,0.85],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.35,0.15],[0.85,0.35],[0.65,0.85],[0.15,0.65],[0.35,0.15]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"Four hypotenuses bound the central square"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.25,0.1],"content":"a"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.6,0.1],"content":"b"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.1,0.4],"content":"b"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.1,0.75],"content":"a"}]},{"op":"text","id":"heading","region":"C1","content":"Pythagorean theorem"},{"op":"equation","id":"rightangles","anchor":{"el":"heading","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"180^\\circ-(\\alpha+\\beta)=90^\\circ"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7fcd35d2-9b0a-4df3-9352-05b7ccf3e0e6","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Each inner corner is ninety degrees because the triangle’s acute angles sum to ninety. The central square therefore has area c squared.","ops":[{"op":"line","id":"hypotenuse","canvas_id":"arrangement","from":[0.35,0.15],"to":[0.85,0.35],"stroke":"solid","label":"c"},{"op":"point","id":"centralarea","canvas_id":"arrangement","at":[0.5,0.5],"label":"c²"},{"op":"equation","id":"areabalance","anchor":{"el":"rightangles","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"(a+b)^2=4\\left(\\frac{ab}{2}\\right)+c^2"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why does the area equation include four copies of ab divided by two?","expected_gist":"There are four congruent right triangles, each with legs a and b and area ab/2."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7fcd35d2-9b0a-4df3-9352-05b7ccf3e0e6","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Expand the outer square’s area and combine the four triangle areas. Both sides contain the same two ab term.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"expanded","anchor":{"el":"areabalance","side":"below","gap":0.09},"latex":"a^2+2ab+b^2=2ab+c^2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"7fcd35d2-9b0a-4df3-9352-05b7ccf3e0e6","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Subtract two ab from both sides. The squares of the legs add to the square of the hypotenuse.","ops":[{"op":"line","id":"secondside","canvas_id":"arrangement","from":[0.85,0.35],"to":[0.65,0.85],"stroke":"solid","label":"c"},{"op":"equation","id":"theorem","anchor":{"el":"expanded","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
